      "question": "What event or condition triggers the scheduling of a vendor meeting — for example, is it calendar-based, performance-threshold-based, or initiated ad hoc by a specific role?",
      "answer": "The meeting is triggered by an improvement request or equipment issue summary. It is initiated based on identified needs rather than a fixed calendar schedule. The equipment engineer prepares the agenda and issue summary first, which then prompts the meeting organizer to schedule the meeting with the vendor."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "Which internal roles are required to attend the vendor meeting, and is there a defined role responsible for facilitating or leading it?",
      "answer": "The equipment engineer and meeting organizer are the key internal roles at the meeting. The equipment engineer leads the discussion of improvement options, while the meeting organizer facilitates by recording decisions and open questions. The manager is involved post-meeting for action-item assignment. The vendor representative also participates."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "How are discussion points and decisions captured during the meeting — for example, through free-form notes, a structured template, or a recording tool?",
      "answer": "The meeting organizer records decisions and open questions during the meeting using meeting notes. The specific format — free-form or structured template — is not specified. Outputs include documented decisions and open questions, which feed into the post-meeting summary stored in a shared document repository."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "Who is responsible for creating the post-meeting summary, and is there a standard format or destination system where it must be stored or sent?",
      "answer": "The meeting organizer is responsible for creating and sharing the meeting summary. It is stored in a shared document repository. The summary includes meeting minutes and the action-item list. A specific standard format beyond these defined outputs is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "How are action items assigned and tracked after the meeting — for example, are they entered into a task management tool, sent via email, or managed through a spreadsheet?",
      "answer": "After the meeting, the manager assigns action-item owners and due dates. Items are tracked using an action-item tracker system. Action-item owners are then responsible for tracking progress. Automated reminders and status tracking are identified as an automation opportunity for this tracking step."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "What criteria or event marks the workflow as fully complete — for example, when all action items are closed, when a sign-off is given by a specific role, or after a defined review period?",
      "answer": "The workflow is not explicitly defined as complete by a specific sign-off or review period. The final activity is action-item owners tracking progress to closure. A missed follow-up deadline is noted as an exception, implying completion depends on all action items being resolved, but a formal closure criterion is not specified."
    }
  ]
}
